### Instruction:
Please implement a function in Java that can detect cycles in a directed graph using depth-first search (DFS) algorithm, without using recursion. The function should return a boolean value indicating whether the graph contains any cycles or not.

You can define the following classes and methods:

1. Graph class:
   - This class represents a directed graph.
   - It should have an integer value variable `numVertices` to store the number of vertices in the graph.
   - It should have a 2D boolean array `adjMatrix` to represent the adjacency matrix of the graph.

   - Method 1: `addEdge(int source, int destination)`:
     - This method takes two integers as parameters: `source` and `destination`, and adds a directed edge from `source` to `destination` in the adjacency matrix.

   - Method 2: `containsCycle()`:
     - This method detects cycles in the graph using depth-first search algorithm without using recursion.
     - It should return a boolean value indicating whether the graph contains any cycles or not.

You need to implement the above classes and methods to pass the given test.

Note: Depth-first search (DFS) is an algorithm for traversing or searching tree or graph data structures. It starts at the root node and explores as far as possible along each branch before backtracking. In the case of a directed graph, DFS can be used to detect cycles by maintaining a visited array to keep track of visited nodes and a recursion stack to keep track of the current path being explored.

### Response:
Here is the implementation of the Graph class and the containsCycle() method using depth-first search without recursion:

```java
import java.util.Stack;

class Graph {
    private int numVertices;
    private boolean[][] adjMatrix;

    public Graph(int numVertices) {
        this.numVertices = numVertices;
        this.adjMatrix = new boolean[numVertices][numVertices];
    }

    public void addEdge(int source, int destination) {
        adjMatrix[source][destination] = true;
    }

    public boolean containsCycle() {
        boolean[] visited = new boolean[numVertices];
        boolean[] recursionStack = new boolean[numVertices];

        for (int i = 0; i < numVertices; i++) {
            if (dfs(i, visited, recursionStack)) {
                return true;
            }
        }

        return false;
    }

    private boolean dfs(int vertex, boolean[] visited, boolean[] recursionStack) {
        visited[vertex] = true;
        recursionStack[vertex] = true;

        Stack<Integer> stack = new Stack<>();
        stack.push(vertex);

        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            int current = stack.peek();
            boolean hasUnvisitedNeighbor = false;

            for (int i = 0; i < numVertices; i++) {
                if (adjMatrix[current][i]) {
                    if (!visited[i]) {
                        stack.push(i);
                        visited[i] = true;
                        recursionStack[i] = true;
                        hasUnvisitedNeighbor = true;
                        break;
                    } else if (recursionStack[i]) {
                        return true;
                    }
                }
            }

            if (!hasUnvisitedNeighbor) {
                stack.pop();
                recursionStack[current] = false;
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```

This implementation uses an iterative approach to perform depth-first search. It maintains a stack to simulate the recursive calls of the original recursive algorithm. The visited array keeps track of the visited nodes, while the recursionStack array keeps track of the current path being explored.

The containsCycle() method initializes the visited and recursionStack arrays and then performs a DFS from each unvisited vertex. If a cycle is found during the DFS, the method returns true. Otherwise, it returns false.

Note that this implementation assumes that the graph is represented by an adjacency matrix. The addEdge() method adds a directed edge to the adjacency matrix.

