Implement a breadth-first search algorithm to find the shortest path between two nodes in a weighted graph in Java.

### Response:
Sure! Here's an implementation of the breadth-first search algorithm in Java to find the shortest path between two nodes in a weighted graph.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class ShortestPathBFS {

    // Graph class to represent the weighted graph
    static class Graph {
        int vertices;
        LinkedList<Edge>[] adjacencyList;

        // Edge class to represent an edge in the graph
        static class Edge {
            int destination;
            int weight;

            public Edge(int destination, int weight) {
                this.destination = destination;
                this.weight = weight;
            }
        }

        public Graph(int vertices) {
            this.vertices = vertices;
            adjacencyList = new LinkedList[vertices];

            // Initialize adjacency list for each vertex
            for (int i = 0; i < vertices; i++) {
                adjacencyList[i] = new LinkedList<>();
            }
        }

        // Function to add an edge to the graph
        public void addEdge(int source, int destination, int weight) {
            Edge edge = new Edge(destination, weight);
            adjacencyList[source].add(edge);
        }

        // Function to perform BFS and find the shortest path
        public void shortestPathBFS(int source, int destination) {
            boolean[] visited = new boolean[vertices];
            int[] distance = new int[vertices];
            int[] parent = new int[vertices];

            // Initialize distance and parent arrays
            Arrays.fill(distance, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
            Arrays.fill(parent, -1);

            // Create a queue for BFS
            Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();

            // Mark the source node as visited and enqueue it
            visited[source] = true;
            distance[source] = 0;
            queue.add(source);

            while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
                // Dequeue a vertex from the queue
                int current = queue.poll();

                // Get all adjacent vertices of the dequeued vertex current
                LinkedList<Edge> edges = adjacencyList[current];

                for (Edge edge : edges) {
                    int destinationNode = edge.destination;
                    int weight = edge.weight;

                    // If the destination node is not visited
                    if (!visited[destinationNode]) {
                        visited[destinationNode] = true;
                        distance[destinationNode] = distance[current] + weight;
                        parent[destinationNode] = current;
                        queue.add(destinationNode);
                    }
                }
            }

            // Print the shortest path from source to destination
            printShortestPath(source, destination, distance, parent);
        }

        // Function to print the shortest path from source to destination
        public void printShortestPath(int source, int destination, int[] distance, int[] parent) {
            System.out.println("Shortest Path from " + source + " to " + destination);
            System.out.println("Distance: " + distance[destination]);

            List<Integer> path = new ArrayList<>();
            int current = destination;
            path.add(current);

            while (parent[current] != -1) {
                path.add(parent[current]);
                current = parent[current];
            }

            Collections.reverse(path);
            System.out.println("Path: " + path);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a graph
        int vertices = 6;
        Graph graph = new Graph(vertices);

        // Add edges to the graph
        graph.addEdge(0, 1, 2);
        graph.addEdge(0, 2, 4);
        graph.addEdge(1, 2, 1);
        graph.addEdge(1, 3, 7);
        graph.addEdge(2, 4, 3);
        graph.addEdge(3, 4, 1);
        graph.addEdge(3, 5, 5);
        graph.addEdge(4, 5, 2);

        // Find the shortest path between two nodes
        int source = 0;
        int destination = 5;
        graph.shortestPathBFS(source, destination);
    }
}
```

This implementation creates a Graph class to represent the weighted graph. The Graph class has an inner Edge class to represent an edge in the graph. It also has a shortestPathBFS function that performs breadth-first search and finds the shortest path between two nodes. The printShortestPath function is used to print the shortest path from source to destination.

