### Instruction:
Write a function to traverse a binary search tree using depth-first search algorithm in Java, without using recursion. Additionally, implement a method to calculate the sum of all the nodes in the tree and return the result.

You may assume that the binary search tree only contains positive integers.

Note: Depth-first search (DFS) is an algorithm for traversing or searching tree or graph data structures. It starts at the root node and explores as far as possible along each branch before backtracking.

You can define the following classes and methods:

1. TreeNode class:
   - This class represents a node in the binary search tree.
   - It should have an integer value variable and left and right pointers to its left and right children respectively.

2. BinarySearchTree class:
   - This class represents the binary search tree and contains methods to traverse and calculate the sum of the nodes.
   - It should have a root pointer to the root node of the tree.

   - Method 1: `insert(int value)`:
     - This method takes an integer value as a parameter and inserts a new node with the given value into the binary search tree.
     - The node should be inserted at the correct position based on the binary search tree property.

   - Method 2: `traverseDFS()`:
     - This method traverses the binary search tree using depth-first search algorithm without using recursion.
     - It should print the values of the nodes in the order they are visited.

   - Method 3: `calculateSum()`:
     - This method calculates the sum of all the nodes in the binary search tree.
     - It should return the sum as an integer value.

You need to implement the above classes and methods to pass the given test.

### Response:
Here is the implementation of the TreeNode class:

```java
class TreeNode {
    int value;
    TreeNode left;
    TreeNode right;

    public TreeNode(int value) {
        this.value = value;
        this.left = null;
        this.right = null;
    }
}
```

And here is the implementation of the BinarySearchTree class:

```java
import java.util.Stack;

class BinarySearchTree {
    TreeNode root;

    public BinarySearchTree() {
        this.root = null;
    }

    public void insert(int value) {
        TreeNode newNode = new TreeNode(value);

        if (root == null) {
            root = newNode;
        } else {
            TreeNode current = root;
            TreeNode parent;

            while (true) {
                parent = current;

                if (value < current.value) {
                    current = current.left;

                    if (current == null) {
                        parent.left = newNode;
                        return;
                    }
                } else {
                    current = current.right;

                    if (current == null) {
                        parent.right = newNode;
                        return;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    public void traverseDFS() {
        if (root == null) {
            return;
        }

        Stack<TreeNode> stack = new Stack<>();
        stack.push(root);

        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode current = stack.pop();
            System.out.print(current.value + " ");

            if (current.right != null) {
                stack.push(current.right);
            }

            if (current.left != null) {
                stack.push(current.left);
            }
        }

        System.out.println();
    }

    public int calculateSum() {
        if (root == null) {
            return 0;
        }

        int sum = 0;
        Stack<TreeNode> stack = new Stack<>();
        stack.push(root);

        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode current = stack.pop();
            sum += current.value;

            if (current.right != null) {
                stack.push(current.right);
            }

            if (current.left != null) {
                stack.push(current.left);
            }
        }

        return sum;
    }
}
```

