### Instruction:
Implement a queue data structure in Java using a circular linked list. The queue should support the following operations:
- enqueue(element): Insert an element to the back of the queue.
- dequeue(): Remove and return the element from the front of the queue.
- peek(): Return the element at the front of the queue without removing it.
- isFull(): Return a boolean value indicating whether the queue is full or not. The queue has a maximum capacity specified at initialization.
- getSize(): Return the current size of the queue.

### Response:
Here's an implementation of a queue data structure in Java using a circular linked list:

```java
public class CircularQueue {

    private Node front, rear;
    private int capacity, size;

    // Node class to represent individual nodes in the circular linked list
    private class Node {
        int data;
        Node next;

        public Node(int data) {
            this.data = data;
            this.next = null;
        }
    }

    public CircularQueue(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.size = 0;
        this.front = null;
        this.rear = null;
    }

    public void enqueue(int element) {
        if (isFull()) {
            System.out.println("Queue is full. Cannot enqueue element.");
            return;
        }

        Node newNode = new Node(element);

        if (isEmpty()) {
            front = newNode;
        } else {
            rear.next = newNode;
        }

        rear = newNode;
        rear.next = front; // Make the circular connection
        size++;
    }

    public int dequeue() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Queue is empty. Cannot dequeue element.");
            return -1;
        }

        int removedElement = front.data;

        if (front == rear) {
            front = null;
            rear = null;
        } else {
            front = front.next;
            rear.next = front; // Update the circular connection
        }

        size--;
        return removedElement;
    }

    public int peek() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Queue is empty. Cannot peek element.");
            return -1;
        }

        return front.data;
    }

    public boolean isFull() {
        return size == capacity;
    }

    public int getSize() {
        return size;
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return size == 0;
    }
}
```

Here's how you can use this queue:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CircularQueue queue = new CircularQueue(5);

        System.out.println(queue.isEmpty()); // Output: true
        System.out.println(queue.isFull()); // Output: false

        queue.enqueue(10);
        queue.enqueue(20);
        queue.enqueue(30);
        queue.enqueue(40);

        System.out.println(queue.getSize()); // Output: 4
        System.out.println(queue.peek()); // Output: 10

        System.out.println(queue.dequeue()); // Output: 10
        System.out.println(queue.getSize()); // Output: 3
        System.out.println(queue.peek()); // Output: 20

        queue.enqueue(50);
        queue.enqueue(60);

        System.out.println(queue.isFull()); // Output: true

        queue.enqueue(70); // Output: Queue is full. Cannot enqueue element.
    }
}
```

In this example, we create a circular queue with a capacity of 5. We then enqueue 4 elements and dequeue the first element. Finally, we enqueue 2 more elements to fill up the queue and try to enqueue another element, which results in an error message indicating that the queue is full.

